# Passkeys setup checklist (backend)

> Generated by `php artisan auth:setup` (the light-it auth package). It lists what the
> package could not do for you, because it never edits your files. Tick each box,
> then **delete this file**. How passkeys work:
> https://github.com/Light-it-labs/lightit-auth-laravel/blob/main/docs/passkeys.md

- [ ] **Run `php artisan migrate`** (creates the `passkeys` table).
- [ ] **Set the relying party** in `.env` (read by `config/passkeys.php`). `PASSKEYS_RP_ID` is
  your frontend's host in production, without scheme or port; `PASSKEYS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` lists
  your frontend URLs, comma-separated. Only `local` and `testing` fall back to localhost; in any
  other environment passkeys answer `500` until both are set:

```dotenv
PASSKEYS_RP_ID=localhost
PASSKEYS_RP_NAME="${APP_NAME}"
PASSKEYS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
```

- [ ] **Set `PASSKEYS_USER_HANDLE_SECRET`** in `.env` (required, no fallback; passkeys answer
  `500` without it). Generate it once with `php -r 'echo bin2hex(random_bytes(32));'` and
  **never rotate it**: changing it invalidates every registered passkey.
- [ ] **Use a shared cache store** in production (the boilerplate's `database` store is fine;
  `array` is not): the registration and sign-in challenges live there.

Check it worked: as a signed-in user, `GET /api/passkeys` returns `200` with an empty list.
